# SpecDev Core SDD Principles

These principles apply to **every** Agent session in a SpecDev-enabled project. They are non-negotiable guardrails for Spec-Driven Development (SDD), inspired by Kiro.

## Spec artifacts are the source of truth

Before implementing or changing behavior, you MUST locate and read the current spec for the feature under work:

```text
.specdev/specs/<feature-name>/
  requirements.md
  design.md
  tasks.md
```

- `<feature-name>` is **kebab-case** (e.g. `user-authentication`).
- If the user names a feature, resolve its folder under `.specdev/specs/`.
- If multiple features exist and the target is unclear, ask once which feature applies.
- If no spec exists and the user wants SDD, offer to start the **requirements** phase before writing code.

You MUST treat the **latest on-disk content** of these files as authoritative — not chat history or assumptions from earlier turns.

## Phase progression (strict order)

Do not skip phases. Do not implement from a prompt alone when SpecDev specs are in scope.

| Order | Phase | Artifact | Gate |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Requirements | `requirements.md` | User approves requirements before design |
| 2 | Architecture & design | `design.md` | User approves design before task breakdown |
| 3 | Task planning | `tasks.md` | User approves tasks before implementation |
| 4 | Implementation | code/tests | One task at a time; mark `[x]` only after user confirms |
| 5 | Verification | tests/properties | Map back to EARS criteria in `requirements.md` |

- **MUST NOT** write or edit `design.md` until `requirements.md` is approved for that feature.
- **MUST NOT** write or edit `tasks.md` until `design.md` is approved.
- **MUST NOT** implement tasks until `tasks.md` exists and the user selects a task (or explicitly overrides SDD for a spike).

When the user explicitly says to skip SDD (spike, prototype, emergency fix), note that override once and proceed — do not silently abandon specs.

## Requirements: EARS notation (strict)

All acceptance criteria in `requirements.md` MUST use **EARS** (Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax):

| Pattern | Form |
| --- | --- |
| Ubiquitous | THE `<system>` SHALL `<response>` |
| Event-driven | WHEN `<trigger>` THE `<system>` SHALL `<response>` |
| State-driven | WHILE `<state>` THE `<system>` SHALL `<response>` |
| Optional | WHERE `<feature>` THE `<system>` SHALL `<response>` |
| Unwanted | IF `<condition>` THEN THE `<system>` SHALL `<response>` |

Each requirement SHOULD include a user story: `As a [role], I want [feature], so that [benefit]`.

- **MUST NOT** replace EARS with vague bullets (“handle errors well”, “be secure”).
- **MUST** number criteria so they can be traced in design, tasks, and tests.

## Code and specs stay in sync

- **MUST NOT** change production code behavior without updating the relevant spec when that behavior is covered by `requirements.md`, `design.md`, or `tasks.md`.
- When implementation diverges from an approved spec, **stop** and either update the spec with user approval or revert the code change.
- **MUST** reference requirement or task IDs/numbers in commit messages or PR descriptions when working on a specced feature (when the user uses git/PRs).

## Task execution discipline

When `tasks.md` exists:

- Execute **one** task at a time unless the user asks for a batch.
- **MUST** read `requirements.md` and `design.md` before implementing a task.
- **MUST NOT** auto-continue to the next checkbox after finishing a task — wait for user review.
- Mark a task `[x]` only after the user confirms completion or acceptance criteria are met.

## Verification

Before calling work “done” on a specced feature:

- Trace changes to EARS criteria in `requirements.md`.
- Prefer **property-based** or example tests that encode acceptance criteria where feasible (see `verifier-agent` when present).
- **MUST NOT** claim full completion if open tasks remain in `tasks.md` unless the user explicitly descopes them.

## Interaction with other SpecDev rules

- **Phase agents** (`requirements-agent`, `design-agent`, `task-planner`, `verifier-agent`) add detail for their phase; this rule always applies.
- **`rule-generator`** governs authoring `.mdc` files — not feature specs.
- On conflict, **phase progression and spec truth** win over speed or convenience.

